am":"CIN","distance":0.6772,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2021,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7106,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Vikings went 10-6 in 2019 and outscored opponents 407 to 303. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 8th in EPA per play and the defense 7th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the lowest 11 personnel usage and a Cover 2 rate that was among the highest in the league.","offense":"The Vikings offense was well above average by EPA per play. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the lowest 11 personnel usage and the fewest receivers on the field in the league. Kirk Cousins ran the passing game, with Stefon Diggs leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Vikings had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 7th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 2nd, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 23rd. Danielle Hunter was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Ifeadi Odenigbo, who had 7 sacks while playing only 33% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had a Cover 2 rate that was among the highest in the league.","special":"The Vikings were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 93% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 23% of the money on the books.","players":"Kirk Cousins played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,603 yards. Targets were spread around, with Stefon Diggs and Dalvin Cook leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Dalvin Cook handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Danielle Hunter led the team with 14.5 sacks and Anthony Harris picked off 6 passes.","coaching":"Kevin Stefanski called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Saints under Klint Kubiak and the 2020 Titans. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Cowboys and the 2021 Colts.","trends":"The offense went from 27th in EPA per play in 2018 to 8th in 2019, a big step forward. The defense moved from 2nd in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 7th in 2019, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was pass rate, which went from 65% to 52%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}